> There are some solutions of this problem but none is really
> satisfying:
> -       flag -qlanglvl=extended; loosing the strong type checking of
>        ANSI C 
> -       using unsigned char or idl_char instead of char in the formal
>        parameter's  definition

I don't think this second solution is all that unsatisfying. Your
compiler never sees the .idl file, only the .h file. The .h file,
which specifies the prototype for the functions you're going to
implement on your server, says "idl_char". So, when you write those
functions, use "idl_char" in the declaration. Similarly, IDL defines
"long" to be 32 its and "short" to be 16 bits. Now, if your machine
defines "long" to be 64 bits, you could get in trouble. Using
"idl_long_int" could avoid this problem.

Nonetheless, it'd be nice if IDL translated "char" into "char" instead
of "unsigned char." Lots of C++ programmers (including myself) learned
of this behavior the hard way when their programs didn't link. If I
were smart, I would have used "idl_char" in my function declarations,
but instead I have used "unsigned char".

Another poster mentioned that DCE 1.1 changes this, and the new
behavior sounds like the right one. But unless all the C++ DCE
programmers are using "idl_char" in their function declarations
instead of "unsigned char", there are lots of C++ programs out there
that won't link when DCE 1.1 comes along.

				greg


