================================================================================

11-Feb-95

This patch fixes the XEmacs 19.11 source distribution kit to
build/run on DEC OSF/1 V3.0.  This patch is applicable ONLY to V3.0,
and is specific to using gcc (I used gcc 2.6.3).  For some unknown
reason, when using the native C compiler or the DEC C compiler, the
resulting xemacs image immediately dumps core.

To install this patch, copy it to the srcdir (xemacs-19.11)
directory as file xemacs-19.11-osf-3.0-patch-1 and type:
	
	% cd srcdir
	% patch -p0 < xemacs-19.11-osf-3.0-patch-1

You should be able to proceed with a normal installation via:
	% ./configure alpha-dec-osf1.3 --with-gcc --with-motif
	% make all
	% make install


BACKGROUND

This patch supersedes the initial one developed by Phil Evans
(pre@alf1.mrc-lmb.cam.ac.uk) on 8-Feb-95 (in comp.emacs.xemacs).
Basically, it takes Phil's patch, cleans up the confusion with
bzero, and makes things compile for --external-widget.  Phil's patch
was based on another patch from Carl Roth (roth@cse.ucsc.edu) that
*finally* made subprocesses work under OSF V3.0.

The confusion with bzero being redefined in lib-src/getopt.c was a
result of _NO_PROTO being defined after including config.h (which
included sys/select.h without _NO_PROTO defined).  Perhaps this was
necessary pre-V3.0, but it no longer is, so I removed the _NO_PROTO
definition in lib-src/getopt.c.

A change in the call to XtSetKeyboardFocus in src/ExternalClient.c has
been included to avoid a compilation error (hence, halting the build)
if you configure with --external-widget.  The compilation error
indicates that XtSetKeyboardFocus requires 2 parameters.  I'm not
positive that supplying NULL as the 2nd required parameter is the
right thing, but it does seem reasonable.  I have NOT tested this fix
because it only affects libextcli_Xt.a and libextcli_Xt.so.1, which I
haven't actually used.

There is a minor cleanup in src/Makefile.in.in,src/m/alpha.h, and
src/s/decosf1-3.h that incorporates the -call_shared and -shared
switches for ld (don't assume "-c" for ld).  I only fixed the DEC OSF/1
relevant sections, but it should be extended to the other systems.

If you have any problems with this patch contact Steve Carney
carney@cern.enet.dec.com.  If you have questions about the past
lossage with subprocesses, try contacting Carl Roth or Phil Evans.


