Hello world,

We want to develop distributed applications for RS 6000 machines based
on DCE RPC and ANSI-C. Believing the IDL compiler description (version
01.03.00.00) the IDL compiler produces ANSI-C code (header file and
stub files). But in spite of the -qlanglvl=ansi flag the xlc compiler
(version 01.03.00.33) produces an error message "Function <rpc_call>
cannot be redeclared". The reason is that data types are interpreted
differently.

example:
error_status_t rpc_call([in] long num, [out, string] char txt[20]); 

The IDL compiler interprets char as unsigned char (macro idl_char ->
ndr_char -> unsigned char). Whereas the xlc compiler interprets it as
signed char. Therefore the function's  declaration in the header file
(produced by idl compiler) isn`t compliant to the function`s
definition. 

There are some solutions of this problem but none is really
satisfying:
-	flag -qlanglvl=extended; loosing the strong type checking of
	ANSI C 
-	using unsigned char or idl_char instead of char in the formal
	parameter's  definition; but some library functions
	(e.g. gets) need parameter of type char. Calling such a
	function with a parameter defined as unsigned char or idl_char
	logically leads to an compiler error. Then I need explicit
	casting; -> circumstantially


If someone knows a better solution, please contact:

beuter@informatik.uni-ulm.de


Thanks

Thomas

-- 

