In <JANSSEN.95Mar20185656@holmes.PARC.Xerox.Com> janssen@parc.xerox.com (Bill Janssen) writes:
>I'm sure the DCE folks can fix this problem.  But you might want to
>look at using ILU instead, which really does use strict ANSI C ...

That hardly seems like a realistic solution.  Or was this a thin excuse
for a non-commercial ad?

IDL char's are defined to be translateable between ASCII and EBCDIC.
As a result, we had to play some games when mapping between "char"
in an IDL file, idl_char, and the C datatype char.  DCE IDL defines
char to be unsigned.  C doesn't define the signedness.  As a result,
when going between IDL parameters and C library parameters, you sometimes
have to add casts.  On some platforms, C char is unsigned and you can
leave off the casts.  For maximum portability, don't.

I suppose ILU doesn't have this problem by only supporting 7bit ASCII.
	/r$


