I'm trying to get httpd working in a chroot environment and encountering
some problems.  As an intermediate task, I'm trying simply to get "ls"
working under chroot, and encountering similar problems. In a nutshell,
here is the setup.

a copy of ls is in the directory /chdir. Also in this directory are
copies of the directories leading to, and the files, /usr/shlib/libc.so,
/etc/group, /etc/passwd, /dev/zero, and probably a few others as well.
So, for example, the file /chdir/usr/shlib/libc.so is a copy of the
file /usr/shlib/libc.so.  According to "odump -Dl", libc.so is the
only shared library ls depends on.  However, executing

	chroot /chdir /ls

gives the message "/ls not found". Executing the same command, but with
a command other than ls which does not make use of shared libraries
(eg. chroot /chdir /hostid, with a copy of hostid in chdir) does work.
Clearly, ls is missing something.  However, I have no idea what it is
missing, nor how to find out.  (On a sun, this process was quite a bit
easier--I would try to execute chroot, and it would tell me, "oh, I can't
find file such-and-such".  The error messages on the DEC are a little
more cryptic :-) )

Oh, yes, as the last line implies, I'm doing this on a Dec Alpha.
It's not my normal environment, so please feel free to point out even
really obvious points.


