I guess the "how do I run XYZ chrooted" Q&A should be in the FAQ.  (Perhaps it
is already.)  Anyway, I tackled this same problem back in November.  After all
the smoke had cleared, I ended up with the following files in the directory to
which the application did the chroot() ...

	etc/group
	etc/passwd
	etc/passwd.dir
	etc/passwd.pag
	etc/resolv.conf
	etc/sia/bsd_matrix.conf
	etc/sia/matrix.conf -> /etc/sia/bsd_matrix.conf
	etc/sia/siainitgood
	etc/svc.conf

I'm not certain that all of them are necessary, but the above list does seem
to be sufficient.  Oh, yeah, be sure that everything in the bin subdirectory
(or anything else in your path) is statically linked.  Or in other words, if
you want to use (for example) "ls", be sure to grab the copy from /sbin and
not the one from /usr/bin.  (There's likely a way to get dynamically-linked
programs to run in the chrooted environment, too, but one would probably have
to put even more stuff there, such as the shared objects, and /sbin/loader.

