>Where I can find DCE RPC source code ?

Here's an old canned message I have.

The following is *not an official statement.*  It is my personal opinion,
based on looking at the distribution.

The free DCE RPC is available as
	ftp.dec.com:/pub/DEC/DCE/PD-DCE-RPC.tar.Z
	ftp.dec.com:/pub/DEC/DCE/rpc_internals.ps.Z
and
	http://www.wsg.hp.com/wsg/Global/Products/PD/PD-DCE-RPC.tar.Z
The sources are 2.3 meg compressed, 7meg uncompressed.


The distribution includes some extensions to pthreads need by DCE, the
RPC client and server runtime, the IDL compiler, the rpcd endpoint mapper,
and a test program called "perf."  The runtime only supports the dummy
"no authentication" RPC; standard DCE security features (authentication,
authorization, privacy, etc.) are not provided in this free release.  The
NSI interface to the DCE namespace (e.g., that lets you map names like
/.:/printers/mine to a particular printer) is not provided.

The code appears to be based on the DCE 1.1 codebase.  I ran a few diffs
to compare some files with what's going out on the 1.1 tapes and found
no differences (other then copyright).

DCE needs threads, specifically it is based on Posix threads, Draft 4.
The RPC runtime isolates threads use pretty well to a handful of macros,
so if you don't have DCE threads it should be possible to port to your
own threads.  Full details on the semantics of DCE threads are available
in the Threads AES, which will soon be available from OSF (contact
direct@osf.org).

You can also buy the DCE Porting and Testing Guide from OSF (<direct@osf.org>)
which covers threads and may help with general building issues such as
all the CPP #define's.

The IDL compiler sources "recursively" call lex and yacc.  To do this they
need to save and restore the lex and yacc state.  You will need to know
something about the internals of your lex/yacc generated code.

Once you've solved those problems, the free DCE package provides you with
a free DCE-compatible package for doing unauthenticated RPCs across clients
and servers.

