In article <3l9bvu$iqs@cnj.digex.net>, hashmi@cnj.digex.net (Atiqullah Hashmi) writes:

|>(Note 102a and 103a here means Transarc DCE 1.0.2a and 1.0.3a).
|>We have transarc DCE. I have a server running 103a and one client with 
|>102a. I built an application on the 103a machine and copied it to the
|>102a machine and trying to run it (I copied the 103a's libdce.so.2 also to 102a
|>machine) there but it says it can't export binding to endpoint map.

|>However, the same application built locally on 102a machine runs fine. Also,
|>this application (built on 102a) runs on a 103a machine fine too.

	Sounds reasonable.

|>My understanding is that a application built on one version should run on 
|>another since they are both compatible versions and part of same cell. 

	You seem to be mixing one version of the shared library with a
	different version of the DCE runtime!  This is not something one should
	do; if your DCE runtime is using the libdce.so then it has a very
	good chance of getting confused when the underlying library is switched.

	Different versions of *DCE* can co-exist in the same cell but the binary
	code that runs on the individual machines may not necessarily be
	backward compatible on another machine.  The *servers* which are
	running may support lower versions of *interfaces* but that is not the
	same as the actual binary executable (or shared library) being
	compatible.

|>Any pointers ?? (We are running Sol 2.3 on all machines)

	Do not attempt to run an application built using a higher version
	of libdce and run on a machine with a lower version of DCE.  If
	definitions have changed then even the shared library of the higher
	version may not be able to compensate for the changes in definitions
	in header files, in general!

